## **English Contemplation**

To say that the world is dreamlike does not mean that the realized person stops seeing it. Forms remain visible, relationships continue, the body performs its duties, and life unfolds according to its natural order.

What disappears is the conviction that these changing appearances possess independent and absolute reality.

Before awakening, the dream controls the dreamer because it is accepted as unquestionably real. After awakening, its images may remain in memory, but their power to bind is gone. Self-knowledge produces a similar inward awakening.

The sage does not deny the world. The sage sees it without granting it a reality separate from Consciousness.

The body is cared for but is no longer mistaken for the Self. Pleasure is experienced but not clung to. Pain is acknowledged but not converted into an identity. Action continues, while the silent understanding remains:

> **“I am the awareness in which the body, mind and world appear.”**

Thus, liberation is not the disappearance of experience. It is the disappearance of bondage within experience.

